     5	# /sdd-spec — Scaffold a Feature Specification
     6	
     7	Scaffold a new Feature Specification using the SDD methodology.
     8	
     9	## Usage
    10	```
    11	/sdd-spec <feature-name> [--type feature|hotfix] [--base-branch <branch>] [-- free-form description and notes]
    12	```
    13	
    14	`--type` / `--base-branch` are explicit overrides for the flow resolved in
    15	§2d — they win over any brainstorm/proposal frontmatter and over the
    16	`WorkKind` mapping (FEAT-466). Omit both to let §2d resolve the flow from
    17	the exploration doc (or default to `feature`/`dev` when none exists).
    18	
    19	## Guardrails
    20	- Always use the official template at `sdd/templates/spec.md`.
    21	- Do NOT write implementation bodies in the spec — but every §3 module MUST
    22	  carry an **Interface Skeleton** (signatures, docstrings, `verified:` anchors;
    23	  see §4 item 6). Bodies belong to task Implementation Blueprints (`/sdd-task`).
    24	- **Feature IDs are unique by construction**: new `FEAT-<NNN>` numbers are
    25	  reserved via `scripts/sdd/reserve_ids.py` (FEAT-387) — a git-native
    26	  compare-and-swap ledger, not a manual "check existing specs" scan — so
    27	  two `/sdd-spec` runs racing each other cannot silently collide on the
    28	  same number. See §5 below. The one documented exception is intentional
    29	  `FEAT-<NNN>` reuse across a deliberate multi-spec split of one
    30	  initiative (e.g. FEAT-380 across `sandbox-hardening`,
    31	  `shelltool-hardening`, `tool-result-compression`) — declared explicitly
    32	  via a `reuse_feature_id` frontmatter field, never a silent fallback.
    33	- If a `.brainstorm.md` exists for this feature in `sdd/proposals/`, use it as input.
    34	- **NEVER re-ask a question that the brainstorm already answered.** Resolved
    35	  answers must be carried forward verbatim, not re-opened. See §2 for the
    36	  resolved-question convention and §3 for what you MAY ask.
    37	- **Always commit the spec file to the current branch** so worktrees can see it.
    38	
    39	## Steps
    40	
    41	### 1. Parse Input
    42	- **feature-name**: slug-friendly kebab-case. If not provided, ask.
    43	- **free-form notes**: anything after `--`, used as Problem Statement seed.
    44	
    45	### 2. Check for Prior Exploration (and carry it forward)
    46	
    47	Look for prior exploration documents in `sdd/proposals/`:
    48	- `<feature-name>.brainstorm.md` → structured options analysis with a Recommended Option.
    49	- `<feature-name>.proposal.md` → discussion output.
    50	
    51	If neither exists, proceed to §3.
    52	
    53	**If a brainstorm exists, you MUST treat it as the authoritative input.**
    54	Do the following in order before writing anything or asking the user anything:
    55	
    56	#### 2a. Map the brainstorm into the spec
    57	
    58	Carry each brainstorm section into the spec per this mapping. This is not
    59	optional — every non-empty brainstorm section below has a target in the spec:
    60	
    61	| Brainstorm section | Spec target |
    62	|---|---|
    63	| Problem Statement | §1 Motivation — Problem Statement (verbatim, condensed only if needed) |
    64	| Constraints & Requirements | §1 Goals + §5 Acceptance Criteria (every hard constraint becomes a checkable criterion) |
    65	| Recommendation + Recommended Option body | §2 Architectural Design — Overview |
    66	| Feature Description → User-Facing Behavior | §2 Overview |
    67	| Feature Description → Internal Behavior | §2 Component Diagram + Integration Points |
    68	| Feature Description → Edge Cases & Error Handling | §7 Known Risks / Gotchas |
    69	| Capabilities (New + Modified) | §3 Module Breakdown (one module per capability as a starting point) |
    70	| Impact & Integration table | §2 Integration Points |
    71	| Code Context (entire section) | §6 Codebase Contract (re-verify every reference — code may have shifted) |
    72	| Libraries / Tools table | §7 External Dependencies |
    73	| Parallelism Assessment | Worktree Strategy section |
    74	| Open Questions (see 2b) | §8 Open Questions (with resolved/unresolved state preserved) |
    75	
    76	Rejected options from the brainstorm are NOT carried into the spec body.
    77	They may be referenced in one line inside §1 Non-Goals if the rejection
    78	excludes something a reader might expect (e.g. *"Runtime fallback-on-failure
    79	was rejected in brainstorm — see proposals/<name>.brainstorm.md Option A."*).
    80	
    81	#### 2b. Parse the Open Questions section — resolved vs. unresolved
    82	
    83	The brainstorm's Open Questions use this convention:
    84	
    85	```
    86	- [ ] Unresolved question — *Owner: name*
    87	- [x] Resolved question — *Owner: name*: <answer text>
    88	```
    89	
    90	- A **`[x]`** checkbox means the user has already answered the question.
    91	  The answer is the text after the final `:` on the same line (or the
    92	  indented lines immediately below, if any).
    93	- A **`[ ]`** checkbox means the question is still open.
    94	
    95	**Rules for resolved (`[x]`) questions — this is the heart of the fix:**
    96	
    97	1. **Do NOT re-ask the user.** Never include a resolved brainstorm question
    98	   in the clarifying-question batch in §3.
    99	2. **Route the answer into the spec body where the decision actually
   100	   applies** — not just into §8. For example:
   101	   - "Default backend when unset → sqlite" → state this in §2 Overview and
   102	     add an acceptance criterion in §5. Do not leave it as an open question.
   103	   - "Binary overflow path declared in `project/conf.py`" → add the config
   104	     key to §6 Configuration References and mention the path in §7
   105	     Patterns to Follow. Do not describe the design as "mingled" or any
   106	     alternative that contradicts the resolved answer.
   107	   - "No TTL in SQL backends" → reflect this in the schema DDL (no
   108	     `expires_at` predicate) and in §7 Known Risks.
   109	3. **Also echo the resolution in §8** as a resolved item so readers can
   110	   audit the decision trail:
   111	   ```
   112	   - [x] <Question restated> — *Resolved in brainstorm*: <answer verbatim>
   113	   ```
   114	   This keeps §8 honest about what was decided and by whom.
   115	4. **If a resolved answer conflicts with your own instinct for the spec**,
   116	   the brainstorm wins. Do not silently override it. If you believe the
   117	   answer is wrong, surface the conflict to the user as a *new* question
   118	   in §3 — do not rewrite the answer.
   119	
   120	**Rules for unresolved (`[ ]`) questions:**
   121	
   122	- Carry them forward into §8 of the spec as `[ ]` items.
   123	- They are fair game for §3 clarifying questions, but only if they
   124	  genuinely block the spec (ones that can be decided during
   125	  implementation should stay as `[ ]` in §8 and not be asked now).
   126	
   127	#### 2c. Show the user the carry-forward summary before asking anything
   128	
   129	Before the clarifying-question round in §3, print:
   130	
   131	```
   132	Loaded brainstorm: sdd/proposals/<feature-name>.brainstorm.md
   133	  Recommended Option: <X — name>
   134	  Resolved questions carried forward (N): <one-line list>
   135	  Unresolved questions remaining (M): <one-line list>
   136	  Clarifying questions I still need to ask (K): <one-line list or "none">
   137	```
   138	
   139	If K is zero, proceed directly to §3b without asking anything.
   140	
   141	#### 2d. Sync the Base Branch (FEAT-145, resolver added FEAT-466)
   142	
   143	Resolve `(TYPE, BASE_BRANCH)` deterministically via
   144	`scripts.sdd.sdd_meta.resolve_flow()` — explicit `--type`/`--base-branch`
   145	flags win over the brainstorm/proposal frontmatter, which wins over the
   146	default (`feature`/`dev`). Unlike a bare `parse()` call, `resolve_flow()`
   147	does NOT raise `FileNotFoundError` when no exploration doc exists — that is
   148	exactly the dev-loop bug path's normal situation (no brainstorm, no
   149	proposal), and it is why this step no longer calls `parse()` directly:
   150	
   151	```bash
   152	META=$(python -c "
   153	from pathlib import Path
   154	from scripts.sdd.sdd_meta import resolve_flow
   155	m = resolve_flow(
   156	    doc_path=Path('<brainstorm-or-proposal-path>') if '<exploration-doc-exists>' else None,
   157	    type_override='<--type value, or empty string if not passed>' or None,
   158	    base_branch_override='<--base-branch value, or empty string if not passed>' or None,
   159	)
   160	print(m.type, m.base_branch)")
   161	TYPE=$(echo "$META" | awk '{print $1}')
   162	BASE_BRANCH=$(echo "$META" | awk '{print $2}')
   163	```
   164	
   165	**Validation (hotfix/main):** `resolve_flow()` enforces `type='hotfix' ⇒
   166	base_branch='main'` internally (it returns a `FlowMeta`, whose own
   167	cross-field validator raises `ValueError` — this is NOT re-derived as a
   168	separate bash check). The `python -c` call above therefore exits non-zero
   169	and prints a traceback when a `--type hotfix --base-branch dev`-style
   170	combination (or hotfix frontmatter with a non-`main` base) is resolved. On
   171	that failure, abort with the same message as before:
   172	```
   173	⚠️  type='hotfix' requires base_branch='main' (got base_branch='<value>').
   174	   Fix the brainstorm/proposal frontmatter (or --base-branch flag) and
   175	   re-run /sdd-spec.
   176	```
   177	
   178	**Validation (feature/not-main):** `FlowMeta` does NOT reject
   179	`type='feature', base_branch='main'` at the schema layer (that refusal is
   180	intentionally a command-layer guard, FEAT-187) — so this check stays a
   181	manual bash condition after the resolver returns successfully. If
   182	`TYPE == "feature"` and `BASE_BRANCH == "main"`, abort:
   183	```
   184	⚠️  type='feature' cannot base on 'main'. Features land on dev (default)
   185	   or staging (during a release freeze). For changes that must base on
   186	   main, set type='hotfix' in the document frontmatter.
   187	```
   188	
   189	Note: `staging` is a valid `base_branch` for `type: feature` during a release freeze
   190	(FEAT-187). Use `base_branch: staging` when applying stabilization fixes to a
   191	frozen release candidate.
   192	
   193	**Sync:** before scaffolding, switch to the base branch and pull:
   194	```bash
   195	git checkout "$BASE_BRANCH"
   196	git pull --ff-only origin "$BASE_BRANCH"
   197	```
   198	
   199	If the working tree is dirty, abort with:
   200	```
   201	⚠️  Cannot sync <BASE_BRANCH>: working tree has uncommitted changes.
   202	   Stash or commit first, then re-run /sdd-spec.
   203	```
   204	
   205	If `--ff-only` fails, abort with:
   206	```
   207	⚠️  Cannot fast-forward <BASE_BRANCH>. Reconcile manually
   208	   (git pull --rebase or merge), then re-run /sdd-spec.
   209	```
   210	
   211	Carry `TYPE` and `BASE_BRANCH` forward into the spec's frontmatter at §5.
   212	
   213	### 3b. Collaborative Design Research (codex seat — optional, NEVER blocking)
   214	
   215	An independent design opinion over the **accepted exploration document**, taken
   216	*before* you draft §2/§6 so it cannot become a ratification of your own design
   217	(FEAT-545). This step is optional: every failure below is recorded as a skip
   218	reason for spec §9 and the command continues. **This step must never abort
   219	`/sdd-spec`** — `sdd-planner` runs this command unattended.
   220	
   221	**Preconditions (any false ⇒ skip):**
   222	- §2 found `<exploration-doc>` and its status is `accepted` (brainstorm
   223	  `**Status**: accepted`, or proposal frontmatter `status: accepted`).
   224	- `command -v codex` succeeds.
   225	
   226	**Rules (identical to the Adversarial Cross-Check in `.claude/agents/code-reviewer.md`):**
   227	- **Never feed the reviewer your reasoning, draft, or preferred conclusion.**
   228	  The brief carries ONLY the exploration document and verified code anchors.
   229	- **Run it in the background** — a call takes 30 s to 10 min. Do not call it
   230	  per edit.
   231	- **Treat the output as advisory.** Every suggestion gets a disposition:
   232	  `CONFIRM` (fold into §2/§3/§7), `REJECT` (record why), `ESCALATE` (becomes
   233	  a `[ ]` item in §8).
   234	- **Never silently concede and never silently drop** a suggestion.
   235	- **Verify the reviewer's evidence.** Every `affected_paths` entry is checked
   236	  with `test -e`; an unverifiable path ⇒ `REJECT` "path not found".
   237	
   238	> **`agy` (Google Gemini / Antigravity) MUST NOT be used for this seat** — same
   239	> ban and same reason as for code review (`CLAUDE.md`, "Adversarial Second
   240	> Opinion"). With no `codex`, skip; do not substitute another external CLI.
   241	
   242	#### 3b.1 Detect and probe
   243	```bash
   244	REPO_ROOT="$(pwd)"                                   # /sdd-spec always runs from the repo root (§2d)
   245	MODEL="${SDD_DESIGN_RESEARCH_MODEL:-gpt-5.6-luna}"
   246	DR="sdd/state/.design_research/<feature-name>"      # id-independent staging: FEAT-ID is reserved only in §5
   247	mkdir -p "$DR"; SKIP_REASON=""
   248	if ! command -v codex >/dev/null 2>&1; then SKIP_REASON="codex CLI not installed"; fi
   249	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   250	  timeout 120 codex exec --ephemeral --sandbox read-only -m "$MODEL" \
   251	    -c model_reasoning_effort=high --ignore-user-config \
   252	    -o "$DR/probe.txt" "Reply with exactly the single word OK." >/dev/null 2>&1 \
   253	    || SKIP_REASON="model probe failed for $MODEL (rc=$?)"
   254	fi
   255	```
   256	
   257	#### 3b.2 Render the neutral brief (skipped when `SKIP_REASON` is already set)
   258	Write each extracted value below to its own file under `$DR` — `problem_statement.txt`,
   259	`constraints_and_goals.txt`, `recommended_option_or_scope.txt`, `code_context_paths.txt`,
   260	`open_questions.txt`, `question.txt` (plain UTF-8 text, no code fences) — **before** running
   261	the renderer, sourced from:
   262	- `problem_statement.txt` ← brainstorm "## Problem Statement" | proposal "## 1. Synthesis Summary" + §0 Origin quote
   263	- `constraints_and_goals.txt` ← brainstorm "## Constraints & Requirements" | proposal "### 2.2 Constraints Discovered"
   264	- `recommended_option_or_scope.txt` ← brainstorm "## Recommendation" + Recommended Option body | proposal "## 3. Probable Scope" (or "## 3. Hypothesis")
   265	- `code_context_paths.txt` ← the **paths only** (one per line) from brainstorm "## Code Context" | proposal "### 2.1 Localization"
   266	- `open_questions.txt` ← the `[ ]` items of the exploration doc (or "none")
   267	- `question.txt` ← "Given this accepted design intent and these verified code anchors, how would you build it? What is missing, risky, or better done another way?"
   268	
   269	```bash
   270	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   271	  python - "$DR" <<'PY' || SKIP_REASON="brief rendering failed"
   272	import sys
   273	from pathlib import Path
   274	
   275	dr = Path(sys.argv[1])
   276	template = Path("sdd/templates/design_research.prompt.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
   277	names = [
   278	    "problem_statement", "constraints_and_goals", "recommended_option_or_scope",
   279	    "code_context_paths", "open_questions", "question",
   280	]
   281	for name in names:
   282	    value = (dr / f"{name}.txt").read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
   283	    template = template.replace("{{" + name + "}}", value)
   284	assert "{{" not in template, "unfilled placeholder remains"
   285	(dr / "brief.md").write_text(template, encoding="utf-8")
   286	PY
   287	fi
   288	```
   289	FORBIDDEN in the brief: anything you have written for this spec, your
   290	reasoning, this command's text, or a preferred answer. If in doubt, leave it out.
   291	Note: the template's own header comment intentionally spells placeholder names WITHOUT
   292	`{{ }}` braces, precisely so this whole-document `str.replace()` cannot also rewrite the
   293	comment (verified by TASK-3099's dry run, which caught this exact corruption before the fix).
   294	
   295	#### 3b.3 Run codex (capped, synchronous — NOT a background job)
   296	```bash
   297	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   298	  timeout 600 codex exec --ephemeral --sandbox read-only --cd "$REPO_ROOT" \
   299	    -m "$MODEL" -c model_reasoning_effort=high --ignore-user-config \
   300	    --output-schema sdd/templates/design_research.schema.json \
   301	    -o "$DR/suggestions.json" - < "$DR/brief.md" > "$DR/codex.log" 2>&1
   302	  rc=$?
   303	  [ "$rc" -eq 124 ] && SKIP_REASON="codex timed out after 600s"
   304	  [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ] && SKIP_REASON="codex exited $rc (see $DR/codex.log)"
   305	fi
   306	```
   307	This call blocks for up to 600s (`timeout 600`, foreground). There is no background/job-control
   308	mechanism here — if you want to do other useful work (e.g. start §4 codebase research) while
   309	waiting, run this step as a separate shell invocation and poll/join it yourself; do not assume
   310	concurrency is provided for you.
   311	
   312	#### 3b.4 Validate and triage
   313	```bash
   314	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   315	  python -c "
   316	import json, sys, jsonschema
   317	s = json.load(open('sdd/templates/design_research.schema.json'))
   318	d = json.load(open('$DR/suggestions.json'))
   319	jsonschema.Draft202012Validator(s).validate(d)
   320	print(len(d['suggestions']), 'suggestions')" || SKIP_REASON="suggestions.json failed schema validation"
   321	fi
   322	```
   323	For each suggestion (when not skipped): verify every `affected_paths` entry
   324	(`test -e <path>`); read the cited spots; decide **CONFIRM / REJECT /
   325	ESCALATE** with a one-sentence reason; write `$DR/triage.md` using the §9
   326	table shape from `sdd/templates/spec.md`. A suggestion with any unverifiable
   327	path is `REJECT — path not found`.
   328	
   329	#### 3b.5 Fold and record
   330	- `CONFIRM` → apply while drafting §2 Overview / §3 modules / §7 notes; the
   331	  §9 row's "Landed in" names the section.
   332	- `ESCALATE` → add a `[ ]` question to §8 (owner: the user); "Landed in" = `§8 Q<N>`.
   333	- `REJECT` → row only.
   334	- Fill spec **§9 Design Research Cross-Check** from `$DR/triage.md`, with
   335	  `Model: <MODEL>` and `Status: completed` — or, when skipped, a single
   336	  line `Status: skipped (<SKIP_REASON>)` and an empty table.
   337	- §6 moves `$DR` to `sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/` and commits it
   338	  with the spec.
   339	
   340	### 3. Ask Clarifying Questions (only what is genuinely missing)
   341	
   342	After §2c, you may ask the user **only** for gaps the brainstorm/proposal did
   343	not cover. Typical legitimate gaps:
   344	
   345	- Spec-level fields that don't exist in a brainstorm (Target version, Author
   346	  attribution if unclear, Status lifecycle preference).
   347	- New `[ ]` open questions that genuinely block the design (not ones that
   348	  can be deferred to implementation).
   349	- Ambiguities discovered during codebase research in §4 (e.g., two plausible
   350	  integration points — which one to use).
   351	
   352	**Forbidden in this step:**
   353	- Re-asking anything that already appeared in the brainstorm's Open Questions
   354	  as `[x]` resolved.
   355	- Re-asking Problem Statement / Constraints / Recommended Option — the
   356	  brainstorm is authoritative on those.
   357	- Asking the user to restate the feature goals in their own words when the
   358	  brainstorm already states them.
   359	
   360	Ask in a single batch so the user answers once and you proceed. If there is
   361	nothing to ask, skip this step silently.
   362	
   363	### 4. Research the Codebase & Build Codebase Contract
   364	Before writing the spec:
   365	- Read existing specs in `sdd/specs/` directory.
   366	- Identify related existing components (AbstractClient, AgentCrew, BaseLoader, etc.).
   367	- Note what can be reused vs. what must be created.
   368	
   369	**CRITICAL — Codebase Contract Construction:**
   370	This step prevents AI hallucinations during implementation. You MUST:
