     1	# /sdd-spec — Scaffold a Feature Specification
     2	
     3	Scaffold a new Feature Specification using the SDD methodology.
     4	
     5	## Usage
     6	```
     7	/sdd-spec <feature-name> [--type feature|hotfix] [--base-branch <branch>] [-- free-form description and notes]
     8	```
     9	
    10	`--type` / `--base-branch` are explicit overrides for the flow resolved in
    11	§2d — they win over any brainstorm/proposal frontmatter and over the
    12	`WorkKind` mapping (FEAT-466). Omit both to let §2d resolve the flow from
    13	the exploration doc (or default to `feature`/`dev` when none exists).
    14	
    15	## Guardrails
    16	- Always use the official template at `sdd/templates/spec.md`.
    17	- Do NOT write implementation bodies in the spec — but every §3 module MUST
    18	  carry an **Interface Skeleton** (signatures, docstrings, `verified:` anchors;
    19	  see §4 item 6). Bodies belong to task Implementation Blueprints (`/sdd-task`).
    20	- **Feature IDs are unique by construction**: new `FEAT-<NNN>` numbers are
    21	  reserved via `scripts/sdd/reserve_ids.py` (FEAT-387) — a git-native
    22	  compare-and-swap ledger, not a manual "check existing specs" scan — so
    23	  two `/sdd-spec` runs racing each other cannot silently collide on the
    24	  same number. See §5 below. The one documented exception is intentional
    25	  `FEAT-<NNN>` reuse across a deliberate multi-spec split of one
    26	  initiative (e.g. FEAT-380 across `sandbox-hardening`,
    27	  `shelltool-hardening`, `tool-result-compression`) — declared explicitly
    28	  via a `reuse_feature_id` frontmatter field, never a silent fallback.
    29	- If a `.brainstorm.md` exists for this feature in `sdd/proposals/`, use it as input.
    30	- **NEVER re-ask a question that the brainstorm already answered.** Resolved
    31	  answers must be carried forward verbatim, not re-opened. See §2 for the
    32	  resolved-question convention and §3 for what you MAY ask.
    33	- **Always commit the spec file to the current branch** so worktrees can see it.
    34	
    35	## Steps
    36	
    37	### 1. Parse Input
    38	- **feature-name**: slug-friendly kebab-case. If not provided, ask.
    39	- **free-form notes**: anything after `--`, used as Problem Statement seed.
    40	
    41	### 2. Check for Prior Exploration (and carry it forward)
    42	
    43	Look for prior exploration documents in `sdd/proposals/`:
    44	- `<feature-name>.brainstorm.md` → structured options analysis with a Recommended Option.
    45	- `<feature-name>.proposal.md` → discussion output.
    46	
    47	If neither exists, proceed to §3.
    48	
    49	**If a brainstorm exists, you MUST treat it as the authoritative input.**
    50	Do the following in order before writing anything or asking the user anything:
    51	
    52	#### 2a. Map the brainstorm into the spec
    53	
    54	Carry each brainstorm section into the spec per this mapping. This is not
    55	optional — every non-empty brainstorm section below has a target in the spec:
    56	
    57	| Brainstorm section | Spec target |
    58	|---|---|
    59	| Problem Statement | §1 Motivation — Problem Statement (verbatim, condensed only if needed) |
    60	| Constraints & Requirements | §1 Goals + §5 Acceptance Criteria (every hard constraint becomes a checkable criterion) |
    61	| Recommendation + Recommended Option body | §2 Architectural Design — Overview |
    62	| Feature Description → User-Facing Behavior | §2 Overview |
    63	| Feature Description → Internal Behavior | §2 Component Diagram + Integration Points |
    64	| Feature Description → Edge Cases & Error Handling | §7 Known Risks / Gotchas |
    65	| Capabilities (New + Modified) | §3 Module Breakdown (one module per capability as a starting point) |
    66	| Impact & Integration table | §2 Integration Points |
    67	| Code Context (entire section) | §6 Codebase Contract (re-verify every reference — code may have shifted) |
    68	| Libraries / Tools table | §7 External Dependencies |
    69	| Parallelism Assessment | Worktree Strategy section |
    70	| Open Questions (see 2b) | §8 Open Questions (with resolved/unresolved state preserved) |
    71	
    72	Rejected options from the brainstorm are NOT carried into the spec body.
    73	They may be referenced in one line inside §1 Non-Goals if the rejection
    74	excludes something a reader might expect (e.g. *"Runtime fallback-on-failure
    75	was rejected in brainstorm — see proposals/<name>.brainstorm.md Option A."*).
    76	
    77	#### 2b. Parse the Open Questions section — resolved vs. unresolved
    78	
    79	The brainstorm's Open Questions use this convention:
    80	
    81	```
    82	- [ ] Unresolved question — *Owner: name*
    83	- [x] Resolved question — *Owner: name*: <answer text>
    84	```
    85	
    86	- A **`[x]`** checkbox means the user has already answered the question.
    87	  The answer is the text after the final `:` on the same line (or the
    88	  indented lines immediately below, if any).
    89	- A **`[ ]`** checkbox means the question is still open.
    90	
    91	**Rules for resolved (`[x]`) questions — this is the heart of the fix:**
    92	
    93	1. **Do NOT re-ask the user.** Never include a resolved brainstorm question
    94	   in the clarifying-question batch in §3.
    95	2. **Route the answer into the spec body where the decision actually
    96	   applies** — not just into §8. For example:
    97	   - "Default backend when unset → sqlite" → state this in §2 Overview and
    98	     add an acceptance criterion in §5. Do not leave it as an open question.
    99	   - "Binary overflow path declared in `project/conf.py`" → add the config
   100	     key to §6 Configuration References and mention the path in §7
   101	     Patterns to Follow. Do not describe the design as "mingled" or any
   102	     alternative that contradicts the resolved answer.
   103	   - "No TTL in SQL backends" → reflect this in the schema DDL (no
   104	     `expires_at` predicate) and in §7 Known Risks.
   105	3. **Also echo the resolution in §8** as a resolved item so readers can
   106	   audit the decision trail:
   107	   ```
   108	   - [x] <Question restated> — *Resolved in brainstorm*: <answer verbatim>
   109	   ```
   110	   This keeps §8 honest about what was decided and by whom.
   111	4. **If a resolved answer conflicts with your own instinct for the spec**,
   112	   the brainstorm wins. Do not silently override it. If you believe the
   113	   answer is wrong, surface the conflict to the user as a *new* question
   114	   in §3 — do not rewrite the answer.
   115	
   116	**Rules for unresolved (`[ ]`) questions:**
   117	
   118	- Carry them forward into §8 of the spec as `[ ]` items.
   119	- They are fair game for §3 clarifying questions, but only if they
   120	  genuinely block the spec (ones that can be decided during
   121	  implementation should stay as `[ ]` in §8 and not be asked now).
   122	
   123	#### 2c. Show the user the carry-forward summary before asking anything
   124	
   125	Before the clarifying-question round in §3, print:
   126	
   127	```
   128	Loaded brainstorm: sdd/proposals/<feature-name>.brainstorm.md
   129	  Recommended Option: <X — name>
   130	  Resolved questions carried forward (N): <one-line list>
   131	  Unresolved questions remaining (M): <one-line list>
   132	  Clarifying questions I still need to ask (K): <one-line list or "none">
   133	```
   134	
   135	If K is zero, proceed directly to §3b without asking anything.
   136	
   137	#### 2d. Sync the Base Branch (FEAT-145, resolver added FEAT-466)
   138	
   139	Resolve `(TYPE, BASE_BRANCH)` deterministically via
   140	`scripts.sdd.sdd_meta.resolve_flow()` — explicit `--type`/`--base-branch`
   141	flags win over the brainstorm/proposal frontmatter, which wins over the
   142	default (`feature`/`dev`). Unlike a bare `parse()` call, `resolve_flow()`
   143	does NOT raise `FileNotFoundError` when no exploration doc exists — that is
   144	exactly the dev-loop bug path's normal situation (no brainstorm, no
   145	proposal), and it is why this step no longer calls `parse()` directly:
   146	
   147	```bash
   148	META=$(python -c "
   149	from pathlib import Path
   150	from scripts.sdd.sdd_meta import resolve_flow
   151	m = resolve_flow(
   152	    doc_path=Path('<brainstorm-or-proposal-path>') if '<exploration-doc-exists>' else None,
   153	    type_override='<--type value, or empty string if not passed>' or None,
   154	    base_branch_override='<--base-branch value, or empty string if not passed>' or None,
   155	)
   156	print(m.type, m.base_branch)")
   157	TYPE=$(echo "$META" | awk '{print $1}')
   158	BASE_BRANCH=$(echo "$META" | awk '{print $2}')
   159	```
   160	
   161	**Validation (hotfix/main):** `resolve_flow()` enforces `type='hotfix' ⇒
   162	base_branch='main'` internally (it returns a `FlowMeta`, whose own
   163	cross-field validator raises `ValueError` — this is NOT re-derived as a
   164	separate bash check). The `python -c` call above therefore exits non-zero
   165	and prints a traceback when a `--type hotfix --base-branch dev`-style
   166	combination (or hotfix frontmatter with a non-`main` base) is resolved. On
   167	that failure, abort with the same message as before:
   168	```
   169	⚠️  type='hotfix' requires base_branch='main' (got base_branch='<value>').
   170	   Fix the brainstorm/proposal frontmatter (or --base-branch flag) and
   171	   re-run /sdd-spec.
   172	```
   173	
   174	**Validation (feature/not-main):** `FlowMeta` does NOT reject
   175	`type='feature', base_branch='main'` at the schema layer (that refusal is
   176	intentionally a command-layer guard, FEAT-187) — so this check stays a
   177	manual bash condition after the resolver returns successfully. If
   178	`TYPE == "feature"` and `BASE_BRANCH == "main"`, abort:
   179	```
   180	⚠️  type='feature' cannot base on 'main'. Features land on dev (default)
   181	   or staging (during a release freeze). For changes that must base on
   182	   main, set type='hotfix' in the document frontmatter.
   183	```
   184	
   185	Note: `staging` is a valid `base_branch` for `type: feature` during a release freeze
   186	(FEAT-187). Use `base_branch: staging` when applying stabilization fixes to a
   187	frozen release candidate.
   188	
   189	**Sync:** before scaffolding, switch to the base branch and pull:
   190	```bash
   191	git checkout "$BASE_BRANCH"
   192	git pull --ff-only origin "$BASE_BRANCH"
   193	```
   194	
   195	If the working tree is dirty, abort with:
   196	```
   197	⚠️  Cannot sync <BASE_BRANCH>: working tree has uncommitted changes.
   198	   Stash or commit first, then re-run /sdd-spec.
   199	```
   200	
   201	If `--ff-only` fails, abort with:
   202	```
   203	⚠️  Cannot fast-forward <BASE_BRANCH>. Reconcile manually
   204	   (git pull --rebase or merge), then re-run /sdd-spec.
   205	```
   206	
   207	Carry `TYPE` and `BASE_BRANCH` forward into the spec's frontmatter at §5.
   208	
   209	### 3b. Collaborative Design Research (codex seat — optional, NEVER blocking)
   210	
   211	An independent design opinion over the **accepted exploration document**, taken
   212	*before* you draft §2/§6 so it cannot become a ratification of your own design
   213	(FEAT-545). This step is optional: every failure below is recorded as a skip
   214	reason for spec §9 and the command continues. **This step must never abort
   215	`/sdd-spec`** — `sdd-planner` runs this command unattended.
   216	
   217	**Preconditions (any false ⇒ skip):**
   218	- §2 found `<exploration-doc>` and its status is `accepted` (brainstorm
   219	  `**Status**: accepted`, or proposal frontmatter `status: accepted`).
   220	- `command -v codex` succeeds.
   221	
   222	**Rules (identical to the Adversarial Cross-Check in `.claude/agents/code-reviewer.md`):**
   223	- **Never feed the reviewer your reasoning, draft, or preferred conclusion.**
   224	  The brief carries ONLY the exploration document and verified code anchors.
   225	- **Run it in the background** — a call takes 30 s to 10 min. Do not call it
   226	  per edit.
   227	- **Treat the output as advisory.** Every suggestion gets a disposition:
   228	  `CONFIRM` (fold into §2/§3/§7), `REJECT` (record why), `ESCALATE` (becomes
   229	  a `[ ]` item in §8).
   230	- **Never silently concede and never silently drop** a suggestion.
   231	- **Verify the reviewer's evidence.** Every `affected_paths` entry is checked
   232	  with `test -e`; an unverifiable path ⇒ `REJECT` "path not found".
   233	
   234	> **`agy` (Google Gemini / Antigravity) MUST NOT be used for this seat** — same
   235	> ban and same reason as for code review (`CLAUDE.md`, "Adversarial Second
   236	> Opinion"). With no `codex`, skip; do not substitute another external CLI.
   237	
   238	#### 3b.1 Detect and probe
   239	```bash
   240	REPO_ROOT="$(pwd)"                                   # /sdd-spec always runs from the repo root (§2d)
   241	MODEL="${SDD_DESIGN_RESEARCH_MODEL:-gpt-5.6-luna}"
   242	DR="sdd/state/.design_research/<feature-name>"      # id-independent staging: FEAT-ID is reserved only in §5
   243	mkdir -p "$DR"; SKIP_REASON=""
   244	if ! command -v codex >/dev/null 2>&1; then SKIP_REASON="codex CLI not installed"; fi
   245	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   246	  timeout 120 codex exec --ephemeral --sandbox read-only -m "$MODEL" \
   247	    -c model_reasoning_effort=high --ignore-user-config \
   248	    -o "$DR/probe.txt" "Reply with exactly the single word OK." >/dev/null 2>&1 \
   249	    || SKIP_REASON="model probe failed for $MODEL (rc=$?)"
   250	fi
   251	```
   252	
   253	#### 3b.2 Render the neutral brief (skipped when `SKIP_REASON` is already set)
   254	Write each extracted value below to its own file under `$DR` — `problem_statement.txt`,
   255	`constraints_and_goals.txt`, `recommended_option_or_scope.txt`, `code_context_paths.txt`,
   256	`open_questions.txt`, `question.txt` (plain UTF-8 text, no code fences) — **before** running
   257	the renderer, sourced from:
   258	- `problem_statement.txt` ← brainstorm "## Problem Statement" | proposal "## 1. Synthesis Summary" + §0 Origin quote
   259	- `constraints_and_goals.txt` ← brainstorm "## Constraints & Requirements" | proposal "### 2.2 Constraints Discovered"
   260	- `recommended_option_or_scope.txt` ← brainstorm "## Recommendation" + Recommended Option body | proposal "## 3. Probable Scope" (or "## 3. Hypothesis")
   261	- `code_context_paths.txt` ← the **paths only** (one per line) from brainstorm "## Code Context" | proposal "### 2.1 Localization"
   262	- `open_questions.txt` ← the `[ ]` items of the exploration doc (or "none")
   263	- `question.txt` ← "Given this accepted design intent and these verified code anchors, how would you build it? What is missing, risky, or better done another way?"
   264	
   265	```bash
   266	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   267	  python - "$DR" <<'PY' || SKIP_REASON="brief rendering failed"
   268	import sys
   269	from pathlib import Path
   270	
   271	dr = Path(sys.argv[1])
   272	template = Path("sdd/templates/design_research.prompt.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
   273	names = [
   274	    "problem_statement", "constraints_and_goals", "recommended_option_or_scope",
   275	    "code_context_paths", "open_questions", "question",
   276	]
   277	for name in names:
   278	    value = (dr / f"{name}.txt").read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
   279	    template = template.replace("{{" + name + "}}", value)
   280	assert "{{" not in template, "unfilled placeholder remains"
   281	(dr / "brief.md").write_text(template, encoding="utf-8")
   282	PY
   283	fi
   284	```
   285	FORBIDDEN in the brief: anything you have written for this spec, your
   286	reasoning, this command's text, or a preferred answer. If in doubt, leave it out.
   287	Note: the template's own header comment intentionally spells placeholder names WITHOUT
   288	`{{ }}` braces, precisely so this whole-document `str.replace()` cannot also rewrite the
   289	comment (verified by TASK-3099's dry run, which caught this exact corruption before the fix).
   290	
   291	#### 3b.3 Run codex (capped, synchronous — NOT a background job)
   292	```bash
   293	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   294	  timeout 600 codex exec --ephemeral --sandbox read-only --cd "$REPO_ROOT" \
   295	    -m "$MODEL" -c model_reasoning_effort=high --ignore-user-config \
   296	    --output-schema sdd/templates/design_research.schema.json \
   297	    -o "$DR/suggestions.json" - < "$DR/brief.md" > "$DR/codex.log" 2>&1
   298	  rc=$?
   299	  [ "$rc" -eq 124 ] && SKIP_REASON="codex timed out after 600s"
   300	  [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ] && SKIP_REASON="codex exited $rc (see $DR/codex.log)"
   301	fi
   302	```
   303	This call blocks for up to 600s (`timeout 600`, foreground). There is no background/job-control
   304	mechanism here — if you want to do other useful work (e.g. start §4 codebase research) while
   305	waiting, run this step as a separate shell invocation and poll/join it yourself; do not assume
   306	concurrency is provided for you.
   307	
   308	#### 3b.4 Validate and triage
   309	```bash
   310	if [ -z "$SKIP_REASON" ]; then
   311	  python -c "
   312	import json, sys, jsonschema
   313	s = json.load(open('sdd/templates/design_research.schema.json'))
   314	d = json.load(open('$DR/suggestions.json'))
   315	jsonschema.Draft202012Validator(s).validate(d)
   316	print(len(d['suggestions']), 'suggestions')" || SKIP_REASON="suggestions.json failed schema validation"
   317	fi
   318	```
   319	For each suggestion (when not skipped): verify every `affected_paths` entry
   320	(`test -e <path>`); read the cited spots; decide **CONFIRM / REJECT /
   321	ESCALATE** with a one-sentence reason; write `$DR/triage.md` using the §9
   322	table shape from `sdd/templates/spec.md`. A suggestion with any unverifiable
   323	path is `REJECT — path not found`.
   324	
   325	#### 3b.5 Fold and record
   326	- `CONFIRM` → apply while drafting §2 Overview / §3 modules / §7 notes; the
   327	  §9 row's "Landed in" names the section.
   328	- `ESCALATE` → add a `[ ]` question to §8 (owner: the user); "Landed in" = `§8 Q<N>`.
   329	- `REJECT` → row only.
   330	- Fill spec **§9 Design Research Cross-Check** from `$DR/triage.md`, with
   331	  `Model: <MODEL>` and `Status: completed` — or, when skipped, a single
   332	  line `Status: skipped (<SKIP_REASON>)` and an empty table.
   333	- §6 moves `$DR` to `sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/` and commits it
   334	  with the spec.
   335	
   336	### 3. Ask Clarifying Questions (only what is genuinely missing)
   337	
   338	After §2c, you may ask the user **only** for gaps the brainstorm/proposal did
   339	not cover. Typical legitimate gaps:
   340	
   341	- Spec-level fields that don't exist in a brainstorm (Target version, Author
   342	  attribution if unclear, Status lifecycle preference).
   343	- New `[ ]` open questions that genuinely block the design (not ones that
   344	  can be deferred to implementation).
   345	- Ambiguities discovered during codebase research in §4 (e.g., two plausible
   346	  integration points — which one to use).
   347	
   348	**Forbidden in this step:**
   349	- Re-asking anything that already appeared in the brainstorm's Open Questions
   350	  as `[x]` resolved.
   351	- Re-asking Problem Statement / Constraints / Recommended Option — the
   352	  brainstorm is authoritative on those.
   353	- Asking the user to restate the feature goals in their own words when the
   354	  brainstorm already states them.
   355	
   356	Ask in a single batch so the user answers once and you proceed. If there is
   357	nothing to ask, skip this step silently.
   358	
   359	### 4. Research the Codebase & Build Codebase Contract
   360	Before writing the spec:
   361	- Read existing specs in `sdd/specs/` directory.
   362	- Identify related existing components (AbstractClient, AgentCrew, BaseLoader, etc.).
   363	- Note what can be reused vs. what must be created.
   364	
   365	**CRITICAL — Codebase Contract Construction:**
   366	This step prevents AI hallucinations during implementation. You MUST:
   367	
   368	1. **If a brainstorm exists**: carry forward its entire `## Code Context` section
   369	   into the spec's `## 6. Codebase Contract` section. Re-verify each reference
   370	   is still accurate (code may have changed since brainstorm).
   371	2. **For every class/module referenced in the spec**: `read` the actual source file
   372	   and record exact class signatures, method signatures (with parameter types and
   373	   return types), and key attributes — with file paths and line numbers.
   374	3. **Verify all imports**: confirm that all referenced imports resolve by
   375	   checking `__init__.py` exports and module structure. Do not assume.
   376	4. **Record what does NOT exist**: if you searched for a plausible module, class,
   377	   or method and it does not exist, add it to the "Does NOT Exist" subsection.
   378	   This is the most effective anti-hallucination measure — it explicitly tells
   379	   implementing agents what NOT to reference.
   380	5. **Include user-provided code**: if the user or brainstorm provided code snippets,
   381	   preserve them as verified references in the contract.
   382	6. **Interface Skeletons (FEAT-545)**: for every §3 module write the public
   383	   signatures and docstrings of what the module adds or changes — no bodies —
   384	   each line that touches existing code carrying `# verified: path:NN`. These
   385	   skeletons are what `/sdd-task` turns into per-task Implementation Blueprints,
   386	   so a name fixed here is not renegotiable later.
   387	
   388	### 5. Scaffold the Spec
   389	1. Read the template at `sdd/templates/spec.md`. The template already contains
   390	   a YAML frontmatter block at the top (FEAT-145).
   391	2. Create `sdd/specs/<feature-name>.spec.md` filled in with:
   392	   - **Frontmatter** at the very top: set `type` and `base_branch` to the
   393	     values resolved in §2d. Do NOT strip the frontmatter. The block must
   394	     match this shape exactly:
   395	     ```yaml
   396	     ---
   397	     type: feature        # or: hotfix
   398	     base_branch: dev     # or: main (mandatory for hotfix)
   399	     # reuse_feature_id: FEAT-<NNN>   # OPTIONAL — only for an intentional
   400	     #   multi-spec split of one initiative (see Guardrails); when present,
   401	     #   skip the reserve_ids.py call below and use this ID verbatim.
   402	     ---
   403	     ```
   404	   - **Feature ID — SKIP ENTIRELY when `TYPE == "hotfix"` (FEAT-466).** A
   405	     bugfix is not a feature and reserves no `FEAT-<NNN>`: ledger ids exist
   406	     for features and the brainstorm → spec → task SDD flow, and a hotfix is
   407	     identified by its **Jira issue key** instead. Do NOT call
   408	     `reserve_ids.py --kind feature` on the hotfix path — write no
   409	     `FEAT-<NNN>` anywhere in the spec, and use the Jira key as the
   410	     document's identity line (`**Jira**: <KEY>` in place of `**Feature
   411	     ID**: FEAT-<NNN>`). This is not an oversight to fix later: it is the
   412	     whole point — the allocator's "current branch must equal
   413	     `--base-branch`" precondition (`reserve_ids.py`, `_assert_safe_to_reserve`)
   414	     would otherwise be
   415	     unreachable for a hotfix that has to reserve on `main` while another
   416	     worktree already has `main` checked out. Skipping reservation removes
   417	     the problem rather than solving it.
   418	
   419	     For `TYPE == "feature"`, **first check whether this slug already owns
   420	     an ID**. A slug has ONE feature ID for its lifetime: re-running
   421	     `/sdd-spec` over an existing spec must REUSE that number, never
   422	     reserve a second one. A second reservation forks the feature's
   423	     identity — the spec and the task index move to the new number while
   424	     the task files, the branch and the worktree keep the old one, and
   425	     `DevelopmentNode._find_feature_slug` (which matches strictly on the
   426	     index header's `feature_id` **inside the worktree**) then finds no
   427	     index at all and silently degrades a whole multi-agent pool to a
   428	     single agent. This is not hypothetical: it happened on 2026-09-01 to
   429	     `formfield-content-type` (FEAT-488 → FEAT-489), and cost a full run.
   430	
   431	     ```bash
   432	     EXISTING=$(python -c "
   433	     from pathlib import Path
   434	     from scripts.sdd.reserve_ids import existing_feature_id
   435	     found = existing_feature_id(Path('.'), '<feature-name>')
   436	     print(found[0] if found else '')")
   437	     ```
   438	
   439	     If `$EXISTING` is non-empty, **use it verbatim as this spec's Feature
   440	     ID and skip the reservation entirely** — say so in the §7 output
   441	     ("reusing FEAT-<NNN>, already owned by this slug"). Only when it is
   442	     empty do you reserve:
   443	     ```bash
   444	     FEAT_ID=$(python -m scripts.sdd.reserve_ids --kind feature --count 1 \
   445	       --base-branch "$BASE_BRANCH" --label <feature-name>)
   446	     ```
   447	     `reserve_ids.py` enforces the same rule itself and exits non-zero
   448	     naming the owned ID, so forgetting this check is a hard failure, not
   449	     a silently burned number.
   450	     On success this prints exactly one `FEAT-<NNN>` line; use it verbatim
   451	     as this spec's Feature ID. `reserve_ids.py` pushes its own ledger-only
   452	     commit to `origin/$BASE_BRANCH` as part of this call (retrying
   453	     internally on a non-fast-forward rejection) and refuses to run if
   454	     tracked files have uncommitted changes besides the ledger file. It
   455	     builds that commit on `origin/$BASE_BRANCH` with git plumbing, so it
   456	     never publishes or discards local-only commits on your branch. If the command exits non-zero, **STOP** and report the error —
   457	     do NOT fall back to hand-computing a number.
   458	
   459	     **Escape hatch — intentional FEAT-ID reuse**: if this spec is a
   460	     deliberate split of an existing initiative across multiple specs (the
   461	     FEAT-380-style pattern — `sandbox-hardening.spec.md`,
   462	     `shelltool-hardening.spec.md`, and `tool-result-compression.spec.md`
   463	     all intentionally share `FEAT-380`), do NOT call `reserve_ids.py`.
   464	     Instead, set `reuse_feature_id: FEAT-<NNN>` in the frontmatter above,
   465	     state the reused ID explicitly, and skip the reservation call
   466	     entirely — this documents the intentional reuse for auditability
   467	     rather than silently reusing a stale number.
   468	   - Today's date.
   469	   - Answers from user (or prior exploration documents).
   470	   - Architectural patterns from your codebase research.
   471	3. When populating §8 Open Questions, apply the resolved/unresolved
   472	   partition from §2b. Resolved items use `[x]` and the carried-forward
   473	   answer; unresolved items use `[ ]`.
   474	4. Before finishing, sanity-check the spec against the brainstorm:
   475	   for every `[x]` resolved question in the brainstorm, search the spec
   476	   body for a passage that reflects the resolution. If you cannot find
   477	   one, you have failed to carry the decision forward — fix the spec
   478	   before committing.
   479	
   480	**Worktree hint (new section in spec):**
   481	Include a `## Worktree Strategy` section in the spec with:
   482	- Default isolation unit: `per-spec` or `per-task`.
   483	- If `per-spec`: all tasks run sequentially in one worktree.
   484	- If mixed: list which tasks are parallelizable and why.
   485	- Cross-feature dependencies: list any specs that must be merged first.
   486	
   487	### 6. Commit the Spec
   488	
   489	> **CRITICAL — Worktrees branch from the current state of the repo.**
   490	> If the spec is not committed, any worktree created later will NOT see it,
   491	> and the `sdd-worker` agent will fail with "no spec found".
   492	
   493	> **CRITICAL — Only commit the spec file. NEVER commit unrelated changes.**
   494	> Other files may be modified or unstaged in the working directory — do NOT
   495	> touch them. Follow the exact sequence below.
   496	
   497	```bash
   498	# 1. Unstage everything first to ensure a clean staging area
   499	git reset HEAD
   500	
   501	# 2. Stage ONLY the spec file (+ the design-research transcript when §3b ran) — NEVER "git add ." / "-A"
   502	git add sdd/specs/<feature-name>.spec.md
   503	if [ -d "sdd/state/.design_research/<feature-name>" ]; then
   504	  mkdir -p "sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research"
   505	  mv sdd/state/.design_research/<feature-name>/* "sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/"
   506	  rmdir "sdd/state/.design_research/<feature-name>"
   507	  git add "sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/"
   508	fi
   509	
   510	# 3. Verify ONLY those paths are staged
   511	git diff --cached --name-only
   512	# Expected: sdd/specs/<feature-name>.spec.md [+ sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/*]
   513	# If ANY other files appear, run "git reset HEAD" and start over
   514	
   515	# 4. Commit
   516	git commit -m "sdd: add spec for FEAT-<ID> — <feature-name>"
   517	```
   518	
   519	### 7. Output
   520	
   521	**`TYPE == "feature"`:**
   522	```
