    10	## Development Environment
    11	
    12	### Package Management & Virtual Environment
    13	
    14	**CRITICAL RULES:**
    15	1. **Package Manager**: Use **`uv`** exclusively for package management
    16	   ```bash
    17	   uv pip install <package>
    18	   uv pip list
    19	   uv add <package>
    20	   ```
    21	
    22	2. **Virtual Environment**: ALWAYS activate before Python operations
    23	   ```bash
    24	   source .venv/bin/activate
    25	   ```
    26	   **NEVER** run `uv`, `python`, or `pip` commands without activating first.
    27	
    28	3. **Dependencies**: Manage all dependencies via `pyproject.toml`
    29	
    30	### Repository layout — this is a uv workspace
    31	
    32	The repo root is the `ai-parrot-workspace` declarator
    33	(`[tool.uv.workspace] members = ["packages/*"]`). **There is no `parrot/`
    34	directory at the repo root.** The core package source root is:
    35	
    36	```
    37	packages/ai-parrot/src/parrot/
    38	```
    39	
    40	Sibling distributions under `packages/`:
    41	
    42	| Directory | Distribution | Contributes |
    43	|---|---|---|
    44	| `ai-parrot/` | `ai-parrot` | the whole `parrot.*` namespace (core) |
    45	| `ai-parrot-advisors/` | `ai-parrot-advisors` | `parrot.advisors` |
    46	| `ai-parrot-embeddings/` | `ai-parrot-embeddings` | backends under `parrot.embeddings` / `.stores` / `.rerankers` |
    47	| `ai-parrot-integrations/` | `ai-parrot-integrations` | `parrot.human`, `parrot.integrations`, `parrot.voice` |
    48	| `ai-parrot-server/` | `ai-parrot-server` | `parrot.a2a`, `parrot.handlers`, `parrot.manager`, `parrot.mcp`, `parrot.server`, … |
    49	| `ai-parrot-visualizations/` | `ai-parrot-visualizations` | `parrot.outputs` assets |
    50	| `ai-parrot-tools/` | `ai-parrot-tools` | top-level **`parrot_tools`** |
    51	| `ai-parrot-loaders/` | `ai-parrot-loaders` | top-level `parrot_loaders` |
    52	| `ai-parrot-pipelines/` | `ai-parrot-pipelines` | top-level `parrot_pipelines` |
    53	
    54	Namespace satellites use PEP 420: their `src/parrot/` has **no**
    55	`__init__.py`, only the subpackage they exclusively own does.
    56	
    57	
    58	## Tool-Centric Architecture
    59	
    60	AI-Parrot's agents interact with the world through tools. When creating tools:
    61	
    62	1. **Location**: Concrete external API/service wrappers live in the
    63	   **ai-parrot-tools** distribution (`packages/ai-parrot-tools/src/parrot_tools/`).
    64	   Only the base machinery — `AbstractTool`, `AbstractToolkit`, the `@tool`
    65	   decorator, `ToolManager`, `working_memory/` — stays in core
    66	   `parrot/tools/`. A `sys.meta_path` finder in `parrot/tools/__init__.py`
    67	   redirects `parrot.tools.<x>` → `parrot_tools.<x>` → `plugins.tools.<x>`
    68	   for any name that is not a core submodule, so legacy import paths keep
    69	   working; prefer the explicit `parrot_tools.<x>` in new code.
    70	2. **Decorator Pattern**: Use `@tool` for simple functions
    71	   ```python
    72	   from parrot.tools import tool
    73	
    74	   @tool
    75	   def get_weather(location: str) -> str:
    76	       """Get the current weather for a location."""
    77	       return f"Weather in {location}: Sunny, 25°C"
    78	   ```
    79	
    80	3. **Toolkit Pattern**: Use `AbstractToolkit` for complex tool collections
    81	4. **Documentation**: Every tool MUST have clear docstrings explaining purpose, parameters, and return values
    82	
    83	## Async-First Development
    84	
    85	AI-Parrot is built on async/await patterns
    86	
    87	## Integration Patterns
    88	
    89	AI-Parrot supports multiple integration methods:
    90	
    91	### 1. A2A (Agent-to-Agent)
    92	Native protocol for agent discovery and communication
    93	
    94	### 2. MCP (Model Context Protocol)
    95	Expose agents as MCP servers or consume external MCP servers
    96	
    97	### 3. OpenAPI Integration
    98	Consume any OpenAPI spec as a dynamic toolkit using `OpenAPIToolkit`
    99	
   100	## Non-Negotiable Rules
   101	
   102	### Environment
   103	- Package manager: `uv` exclusively (`uv add`, `uv pip install`)
   104	- ALWAYS activate venv before any command: `source .venv/bin/activate`
   105	- NEVER run python/uv/pip without activating first
   106	
   107	### Code Standards
   108	- All functions and classes: Google-style docstrings + strict type hints
   109	- Pydantic models for all data structures
   110	- async/await throughout — no blocking I/O in async contexts
   111	- Logger (`self.logger`) instead of print statements
   112	
   113	### Workflow: Think → Act → Reflect
   114	1. For complex tasks: create plan in `artifacts/plan_[task_id].md` first
   115	2. Implement incrementally
   116	3. Run `pytest` after ANY logic change — no exceptions
   117	4. Save evidence to `artifacts/logs/`
   118	
   119	### Security
   120	- Never commit API keys — use environment variables
   121	- Never run `rm -rf` or system-level deletions
   122	- No form submissions or logins without user approval
   123	
   124	### Adversarial Second Opinion
   125	
   126	Use an external CLI agent as an independent perspective for adversarial
   127	code reviews, design opinions, brainstorming, research cross-checks, and
   128	implementation sanity checks. The reviewer is **`codex` (OpenAI)**.
   129	
   130	> **`agy` (Google Gemini / Antigravity) MUST NOT be used as a reviewer.**
   131	> It was removed as an option on 2026-09-01 after it returned a fabricated
   132	> review: invented a 188-test pytest run with test names that do not exist
   133	> in the branch under review, then terminated with `Error: timeout waiting
   134	> for response`. A reviewer that hallucinates passing evidence is worse
   135	> than no reviewer, because its output reads like corroboration. Do not
   136	> re-add it, and do not fall back to it when `codex` is unavailable — when
   137	> there is no external reviewer, say so and rely on a Claude subagent.
   138	>
   139	> This bans `agy` **as a reviewer only**. It is unrelated to the
   140	> `google_coding` dev-loop backend, which drives the same `agy` binary as a
   141	> *coding* agent (`dispatchers/google_coding.py`) and is untouched.
   142	
   143	Rules:
   144	- Never feed the reviewer your reasoning, justification, or preferred
   145	  conclusion. Give it only the diff, the requirement, and the question.
   146	  Supplying your conclusions produces ratification, not review.
   147	- Treat reviewer output as advisory. For every substantive finding,
   148	  explicitly mark it as `CONFIRM` (adopt), `REJECT` (with reason), or
   149	  `ESCALATE`.
   150	- Never silently concede to the reviewer and never silently drop a finding.
   151	- Run each reviewer call as a full background agent session. Typical runtime
   152	  is 30 seconds to 2 minutes; do not call it per edit or from hooks.
   153	- For parallel perspective, use one Claude subagent and one background
   154	  reviewer session with the same neutral brief, then synthesize agreements
   155	  and disagreements.
   156	- **Verify the reviewer's evidence before believing it.** If it claims a
   157	  test run, a file, or a symbol, spot-check that the thing exists. Treat an
   158	  unverifiable claim as no finding at all, and say the review was
   159	  unusable rather than reporting it as a pass.
   160	
   161	**Detection:**
   162	```bash
   163	if command -v codex &>/dev/null; then REVIEWER="codex"
   164	else echo "No external reviewer CLI found"; fi
   165	```
   166	
   167	#### codex commands
   168	```bash
   169	# Reviews
   170	codex exec review --uncommitted
   171	codex exec review --base dev
   172	codex exec review --commit <sha>
   173	
   174	# Opinions, brainstorming, and cross-checks
   175	codex exec --sandbox read-only -o <scratch-file> "<neutral brief>"
   176	
   177	# Follow-up in the same Codex session
   178	codex exec resume --last "<question>"
   179	
   180	# Image generation / mockups / wireframes (codex-only)
   181	codex exec --sandbox workspace-write -o <out.txt> \
   182	  "Generate an image: <description>. Save as <name>.png"
   183	```
   184	
   185	#### Design research at spec time (FEAT-545)
   186	
   187	The same codex seat gives an **independent design opinion** in `/sdd-spec`
   188	§3b, over the *accepted* brainstorm/proposal only — never over the spec
   189	draft. Model: `${SDD_DESIGN_RESEARCH_MODEL:-gpt-5.6-luna}` with
   190	`-c model_reasoning_effort=high` and `--ignore-user-config` (the operator's
   191	`~/.codex/config.toml` must not swap the model silently). The pass is
   192	**optional and never blocking**: no `codex`, failed probe, timeout or invalid
   193	output ⇒ spec §9 reads `Status: skipped (<reason>)` and the command continues
   194	(`sdd-planner` runs it unattended). Every suggestion is triaged
   195	`CONFIRM` / `REJECT` / `ESCALATE` in spec **§9 Design Research Cross-Check**;
   196	the transcript is committed under `sdd/state/<FEAT-ID>/design_research/`.
   197	The `agy` ban above applies to this seat too.
