   292	claude --agent sdd-worker --model sonnet --verbose
   293	```
   294	
   295	### Cleanup
   296	
   297	```bash
   298	# After PR merge
   299	git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/<name>
   300	# or prune all dead worktrees
   301	git worktree prune
   302	```
   303	
   304	### .gitignore
   305	
   306	```gitignore
   307	.claude/worktrees/
   308	```
   309	
   310	## SDD Auto-Commit Rule
   311	
   312	> **CRITICAL**: Every SDD command that creates or modifies files MUST commit
   313	> them on the appropriate branch before finishing. Uncommitted files are
   314	> invisible to worktrees and other sessions.
   315	
   316	| Command | What it commits | Where (FEAT-145) |
   317	|---------|-----------------|------------------|
   318	| `/sdd-brainstorm` | `sdd/proposals/<n>.brainstorm.md` (with frontmatter) | `base_branch` |
   319	| `/sdd-proposal`   | `sdd/proposals/<n>.proposal.md` (with frontmatter)  | `base_branch` |
   320	| `/sdd-spec`       | `sdd/specs/<n>.spec.md` (with frontmatter) + a `reserve_ids.py` FEAT-ID reservation commit to `sdd/tasks/.id_ledger.json` (FEAT-387) | `base_branch` |
   321	| `/sdd-task`       | `sdd/tasks/index/<feature>.json` + `sdd/tasks/active/TASK-*` + a `reserve_ids.py` TASK-ID reservation commit to `sdd/tasks/.id_ledger.json` (FEAT-387) | `base_branch` |
   322	| `/sdd-start`      | Per-spec index status update + implementation code  | worktree (feature branch) |
   323	| `/sdd-done`       | Verification stamp on per-spec index (committed on feature branch); merges feature → `base_branch` | worktree (feature branch), merged to `base_branch` by Step 9 |
   324	
   325	Commit message convention:
   326	```
   327	sdd: <action> for <feature-name>
   328	```
   329	
   330	**Note (FEAT-466)**: the `/sdd-spec` and `/sdd-task` reservation commits in
   331	the table above do **not** occur for `type: hotfix`. A bugfix is not a
   332	feature and reserves no `FEAT-<NNN>`/`TASK-<NNN>` id — `/sdd-spec` skips
   333	`reserve_ids.py --kind feature` and `/sdd-task` is normally skipped
   334	entirely (the single-agent dev-loop path handles a hotfix directly from the
   335	spec). The hotfix's identity is its Jira issue key instead; see
   336	`sdd/specs/dev-loop-run-fidelity.spec.md` §3 Module 2.
   337	
   338	**Note (FEAT-145)**: `/sdd-start` no longer needs to `cd` back to the main
   339	repo to update SDD state — per-spec indexes mean each feature owns its own
   340	index file, so the worktree's commit covers code AND state in one stroke.
   341	The merge in `/sdd-done` brings them to `base_branch` atomically.
   342	
   343	**Note (FEAT-387)**: `sdd/tasks/.id_ledger.json` is a git-tracked
   344	compare-and-swap counter for `TASK-<NNN>`/`FEAT-<NNN>` numbers, allocated
   345	via `scripts/sdd/reserve_ids.py` (not scanned-and-incremented by hand). Its
   346	reservation commit is independent — pushed to `base_branch` immediately by
   347	`reserve_ids.py` itself, BEFORE the calling command's own task/spec files
   348	are written, never bundled into the same commit. `scripts/sdd/
   349	check_id_collisions.py` is an independent, read-only backstop wired into
   350	CI that catches any `TASK-<NNN>` collision that still slips through. See
   351	`sdd/WORKFLOW.md` ("TASK/FEAT ID Allocation") for full details.
   352	
   353	## Isolation Model
   354	
   355	Worktrees isolate **features** from each other. Tasks within a feature run
   356	sequentially in the same worktree via `/sdd-start TASK-<NNN>`.
   357	
   358	```
   359	Terminal 1 (in .claude/worktrees/feat-007):     Terminal 2 (in .claude/worktrees/feat-008):
   360	  /sdd-start TASK-001 → commit                   /sdd-start TASK-010 → commit
   361	  /sdd-start TASK-002 → commit (sees 001)         /sdd-start TASK-011 → commit
   362	  /sdd-start TASK-003 → commit (sees 001+2)       /sdd-start TASK-012 → commit
   363	  push, PR against dev                            push, PR against dev
   364	```
   365	
   366	## Typical Workflow
   367	
   368	```bash
   369	# 1. Ensure you're on dev with latest
   370	git checkout dev && git pull origin dev
   371	
   372	# 2. Create and approve a spec (committed to dev automatically)
   373	/sdd-spec videoreel-visual-changes -- ...
   374	/sdd-task sdd/specs/videoreel-visual-changes.spec.md
   375	
   376	# 3. Create worktree from dev
   377	git worktree add -b feat-014-videoreel-visual-changes \
   378	  .claude/worktrees/feat-014 HEAD
   379	
   380	# 4. Enter worktree and work
   381	cd .claude/worktrees/feat-014
   382	
   383	# Manual (task-by-task):
   384	claude
   385	/sdd-start TASK-069
   386	/sdd-start TASK-070
   387	/sdd-done FEAT-014
   388	
   389	# Or autonomous:
   390	claude --agent sdd-worker --dangerously-skip-permissions --model sonnet --verbose
   391	/sdd-done FEAT-014
   392	
   393	# 5. Push and PR
   394	git push origin feat-014-videoreel-visual-changes
   395	# Create PR against dev
   396	
   397	# 6. Cleanup after merge
   398	cd ~/proyectos/...   # back to main repo
   399	git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/feat-014
   400	```
   401	
   402	## Autonomous Agent (`sdd-worker`)
   403	
   404	The `sdd-worker` agent (`.claude/agents/sdd-worker.md`) implements all tasks for
   405	a feature sequentially. Launch it **inside** a manually-created worktree:
   406	
   407	```bash
   408	cd .claude/worktrees/<feature-worktree>
   409	claude --agent sdd-worker --model sonnet --verbose
   410	```
   411	
   412	Key properties: uses Sonnet, implements EXACTLY what tasks
   413	specify (no redesigns), commits after each task.
   414	
   415	For background execution:
   416	```bash
   417	cd .claude/worktrees/feat-014
   418	tmux new -s feat-014 \
   419	  "claude --agent sdd-worker --model sonnet --verbose"
   420	# Ctrl+B, D to detach — tmux attach -t feat-014 to reconnect
   421	```
   422	
   423	## Task Index Schema (FEAT-145 — per-spec)
   424	
   425	Each feature has its own per-spec index at `sdd/tasks/index/<feature-slug>.json`.
   426	The header carries flow metadata cached from the spec frontmatter; the
   427	`tasks[]` array is local to that feature only.
   428	
   429	```json
   430	{
   431	  "feature": "<feature-slug>",
   432	  "feature_id": "FEAT-<NNN>",
   433	  "spec": "sdd/specs/<feature-slug>.spec.md",
   434	  "type": "feature",
   435	  "base_branch": "dev",
   436	  "created_at": "<ISO-8601>",
   437	  "completed_at": null,
   438	  "tasks": [
   439	    {
   440	      "id": "TASK-<NNN>",
   441	      "feature_id": "FEAT-<NNN>",
   442	      "feature": "<feature-slug>",
   443	      "status": "pending",
   444	      "depends_on": [],
   445	      "...": "..."
   446	    }
   447	  ]
   448	}
   449	```
   450	
   451	Both `feature_id` and `feature` must be present on every task entry.
   452	Commands resolve features by matching either field (exact, numeric suffix,
   453	or substring) against the per-spec index headers.
   454	
   455	**Migration history**: the legacy `sdd/tasks/.index.json` monolith was
   456	split per-spec by `scripts/sdd/migrate_index.py`. The monolith is preserved
   457	as a historical artifact and ignored by all FEAT-145 commands. Tasks the
   458	migration could not attribute to a feature live in
   459	`sdd/tasks/index/_orphans.json` and are surfaced (but not assigned) by
   460	`/sdd-status` / `/sdd-next`.
   461	
   462	Authoritative reference: `sdd/specs/sdd-flow-types-and-per-spec-index.spec.md`
   463	(FEAT-145).
   464	
   465	> **Heads-up**: `.gitignore` has a global `templates/` rule (line 245).
   466	> The three `sdd/templates/*.md` files were already tracked before the
   467	> rule landed, so they remain editable. If you ever need to add a NEW
   468	> template file, you must `git add -f` it and consider tightening the
   469	> ignore pattern.
   470	
   471	### When NOT to Use Worktrees
   472	
   473	- **Hotfixes on `main`**: Work directly on `main` or a short-lived `hotfix/*` branch.
   474	- **Documentation-only changes**: No code conflicts possible, work on `dev` directly.
   475	- **Single-task features**: If a spec has only one task, a worktree adds overhead
   476	  with no benefit. Work directly on a feature branch.
   477	- **Exploratory brainstorming**: `/sdd-brainstorm` doesn't produce code — no worktree needed.
   478	- **Quick bug fixes**: If the fix is a single commit, skip the worktree ceremony.
   479	
   480	<!-- parrot:wiki:begin -->
   481	## Codebase Knowledge Graph (LLM Wiki)
   482	
   483	This repository maintains a machine-first knowledge graph of the
   484	codebase (pages + typed edges over a local SQLite plane, built by
   485	`wikitoolkit build`). For ANY question about the codebase — where
   486	something lives, how modules relate, what a subsystem does — you MUST
   487	run a scoped wiki query FIRST, before Grep/Glob/Read or any shell
   488	search (`grep`/`rg`/`find`/`cat` via Bash):
   489	
   490	- `wikitoolkit query "<question>"` — token-budgeted, ranked page
   491	  stubs for a scoped question. ALWAYS start here.
   492	- `wikitoolkit page <id>` — read one page in full (file summaries,
   493	  API outlines, content). Use the ids returned by `query`.
   494	- `wikitoolkit related <id>` — follow typed edges (`contains`,
   495	  `references`) to neighbouring files/modules.
   496	- `wikitoolkit status` — plane statistics and staleness.
   497	- `wikitoolkit build` — refresh the graph after large changes
   498	  (a git post-commit hook may already keep it fresh).
   499	
   500	These same operations are also exposed as native MCP tools —
   501	`wiki_query`, `wiki_page`, `wiki_related`, `wiki_remember`, `wiki_note`,
   502	`wiki_status` — via the `wikitoolkit` MCP stdio server registered in
   503	this repo's `.mcp.json` (FEAT-403). If they appear in your tool list,
   504	prefer calling them directly; they have equal standing with Grep/Read
   505	at tool-selection time instead of competing via a Bash-invoked CLI.
   506	
   507	**Query discipline** (avoids the two most common ways the wiki
   508	"fails" — which are usually caller error, not missing coverage):
   509	
   510	1. **Query for the *thing*, not for your *hypothesis* about it.** The
   511	   ranking is lexical — extra concept words steer it toward those
   512	   concepts. To locate a class or feature, name the symbol/module/
   513	   subsystem you want (`"attestation model service"`), not your theory
   514	   about where it might live.
   515	2. **Follow the thread before falling back.** If a result scores low
   516	   or names a parent module, resolve it with `wikitoolkit page <id>`
   517	   or `wikitoolkit related <id>` — one hop usually lands the real
   518	   page. Do NOT jump to grep just because the first `query` didn't
   519	   rank the exact page first.
   520	
   521	Only fall back to Grep/Glob/Read (or shell search) once a clean query
   522	*and* a page/related follow-up have genuinely come up empty — and say
   523	so before you do. Consider `wikitoolkit build` if results look stale.
   524	
   525	**Saving knowledge (persistent memory).** The wiki is also your
   526	durable memory — what you save here survives this session and is
   527	found by future `wikitoolkit query` calls ("the agent forgets, the
   528	graph does not"). When you learn a durable fact, make a decision, or
   529	extract a lesson worth keeping, SAVE it:
   530	
   531	- `wikitoolkit remember "<fact>" --category [note|decision|lesson|concept]
   532	  [--title "<short title>"] [--link <page_id> --rel <relation>]` —
   533	  file new knowledge (idempotent: same title+category updates the
   534	  existing memory). Link it to the pages it is about.
   535	- `wikitoolkit note <page_id> "<text>"` — append an attributed,
   536	  dated note to an existing page.
   537	- `wikitoolkit link <src_id> <dst_id> --rel <relation>` — connect
   538	  two pages with a typed, asserted edge.
   539	- `wikitoolkit memories` — list saved memories;
   540	  `wikitoolkit audit` — the attributed write log.
   541	
   542	Save selectively: durable decisions, gotchas, and cross-file
   543	relationships — not session chatter. Every write is attributed and
   544	auditable.
   545	
   546	The `/parrotwiki` command wraps these (e.g. `/parrotwiki query how
   547	does ingest work`, `/parrotwiki remember <fact>`, `/parrotwiki --wiki`
   548	to export a human-readable markdown wiki).
   549	<!-- parrot:wiki:end -->

